  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-042$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A trusted executive says people believe disagreement with you has career consequences even though you never intend that. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the perception as consequential evidence, seek examples and broader feedback, examine downstream decisions, change behavior and systems, and demonstrate over time that good-faith challenge is safe.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-017$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You anticipate that upcoming holiday seasonal demand will increase customer ticket volume by 50% in six weeks. How do you prepare your team's workflow?$qb$, $qb$Forecast resource requirements, draft an updated operational schedule, build auto-response templates, and present a capacity plan to leadership.$qb$, $qb$Monitor incoming ticket volumes weekly and hire temporary contractors if daily tickets exceed standard capacity limits.$qb$, $qb$Request immediate approval for additional department overtime budget starting next month to handle the expected workload.$qb$, $qb$Advise customer service agents to streamline ticket response lengths during the holiday season to maintain resolution speed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until ticket volume increases to evaluate whether existing staff can handle the workload without structural changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-018$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A cross-departmental project is 90% complete, but the remaining 10% involves tedious final verification steps that team members are putting off. How do you drive completion?$qb$, $qb$Break down the final verification steps into discrete task assignments, establish clear daily check-ins, and actively remove lingering bottlenecks.$qb$, $qb$Remind the project team during the daily stand-up of the official project completion deadline.$qb$, $qb$Take on the remaining 10% verification tasks yourself to guarantee that the project finishes on time.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the project stall to department managers, requesting that they enforce task completion with their staff.$qb$, $qb$Mark the project as complete in tracking systems while managing remaining verification steps as post-launch maintenance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-019$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are writing a standard operating procedure (SOP) for a new data reconciliation task that junior staff will perform. How do you structure the document?$qb$, $qb$Write concise, step-by-step instructions with visual screenshots, clearly state edge-case exception rules, and test the SOP with a junior peer.$qb$, $qb$Draft a detailed narrative explaining the background, logic, and operational goals of the new reconciliation task.$qb$, $qb$Provide bullet-point guidelines covering main steps and encourage staff to ask questions whenever they encounter edge cases.$qb$, $qb$Record a quick video walkthrough of the process and upload it to the shared drive as the primary reference guide.$qb$, $qb$Adapt an existing general department guide by adding a brief summary section specific to the reconciliation task.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-020$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have three high-priority tasks due by the end of the week, along with multiple recurring daily administrative duties. How do you manage your schedule?$qb$, $qb$Block dedicated focus time for high-priority tasks on your calendar, batch administrative tasks into fixed windows, and track progress daily.$qb$, $qb$Focus on completing administrative tasks first each morning to clear your schedule for high-priority work in the afternoons.$qb$, $qb$Work on high-priority tasks continuously and address administrative duties only when flagged by team members.$qb$, $qb$Extend daily working hours temporarily to ensure all administrative duties and high-priority deliverables are finished on schedule.$qb$, $qb$Request that administrative tasks be reassigned to junior team members for the week so you can focus on priority deliverables.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-040$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Continuous chat notifications and non-urgent email alerts frequently interrupt your deep analytical work during peak hours. How do you manage focus?$qb$, $qb$Set scheduled async communication windows, communicate focus blocks to your team, turn off non-critical alerts, and execute deep work intentionally.$qb$, $qb$Respond to chat notifications immediately as they arrive to keep your inbox clear while working on analysis.$qb$, $qb$Check emails every 15 minutes while attempting to work on analytical tasks simultaneously.$qb$, $qb$Mute chat notifications for the entire day and respond to all messages after business hours.$qb$, $qb$Inform your supervisor that communication volume is preventing you from completing daily assignments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-063$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product launch is eight weeks away. One approval normally takes two weeks, and the approver will be unavailable for one of those weeks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from launch, secure the approval window early, prepare decision-ready material in advance, and define a backup path if timing slips.$qb$, $qb$Submit the approval request one week earlier than usual.$qb$, $qb$Continue the normal plan and ask for urgent approval if the approver is away.$qb$, $qb$Delay all other launch work until the approval is secured.$qb$, $qb$Assume another manager can approve if the normal approver is unavailable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-064$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project is marked 95% complete. The remaining tasks are documentation, user confirmation, and one small defect that does not block use. Team attention is moving to a new project. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define explicit closure criteria, assign owners and dates for the remaining items, resolve or formally accept the defect, and confirm the intended users can proceed before closing.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to finish the remaining items when they have time between new-project tasks.$qb$, $qb$Close the project because the main functionality is already delivered.$qb$, $qb$Complete the documentation yourself so the project can be closed quickly.$qb$, $qb$Move all remaining items to a general backlog and close the project.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-065$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process incident is resolved after several handoffs. You are asked to document what happened for future reference. What do you write?$qb$, $qb$A clear timeline of key events, decisions, owners, evidence, impact, resolution, and the preventive action agreed.$qb$, $qb$A detailed narrative containing all messages and comments in chronological order.$qb$, $qb$A short summary of the final cause and fix only.$qb$, $qb$Screenshots of the chat thread and ticket history.$qb$, $qb$A note stating that the issue was resolved and no further action is required.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-066$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have two important deliverables due Friday, routine daily work, and a new request requiring half a day. The new request is useful but not urgent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect time for the committed deliverables, schedule routine work efficiently, and negotiate a realistic time for the new request rather than silently overloading the week.$qb$, $qb$Accept the new request and plan to work longer on Thursday and Friday.$qb$, $qb$Do the new request first so it does not remain in your queue.$qb$, $qb$Delay routine daily work until next week.$qb$, $qb$Ask a junior colleague to take over your routine duties without checking priorities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-086$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your role requires detailed analysis, routine approvals, and frequent team messages. You are making more mistakes because you switch tasks constantly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create protected focus blocks for high-concentration work, batch routine approvals and messages, communicate response windows, and preserve a channel for genuine urgency.$qb$, $qb$Turn off all notifications for the entire day.$qb$, $qb$Respond to messages immediately so they do not remain in your mind.$qb$, $qb$Work on analysis after normal hours when messages stop.$qb$, $qb$Ask teammates to contact your manager instead of you during analysis work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-109$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key project phase starts in six weeks. It depends on data from another team that has missed similar deadlines before. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree the required data, date, owner, and quality expectations early, set interim checkpoints, and create a contingency for partial or late data.$qb$, $qb$Send an early reminder two weeks before the data is due.$qb$, $qb$Add extra time after the expected data date.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the other team misses the deadline before escalating.$qb$, $qb$Build the final plan assuming the data will arrive on time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-110$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer issue is resolved technically, but the customer has not confirmed whether the solution works in their environment. The ticket is approaching an internal closure target. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Seek customer verification against the agreed success condition, make a reasonable follow-up plan, and close only under the defined policy if confirmation cannot be obtained.$qb$, $qb$Close the ticket because the technical team verified the fix.$qb$, $qb$Leave the ticket open indefinitely until the customer responds.$qb$, $qb$Ask the account manager to close the ticket.$qb$, $qb$Close it and reopen later if the customer complains.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-111$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team agrees to a temporary exception to a normal process for one customer. What do you document?$qb$, $qb$The exception, reason, scope, owner, approval, risks, compensating controls, start and end date, and review point.$qb$, $qb$Only the approving manager and the customer's name.$qb$, $qb$A copy of the meeting chat where the exception was discussed.$qb$, $qb$The exception and reason, without an end date.$qb$, $qb$Nothing formal because the exception is temporary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-112$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have a fixed afternoon deadline and a morning meeting that is useful but not essential. The meeting organizer asks you to stay for an extra hour. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the deadline, confirm whether your continued presence is necessary, and leave or arrange another way to contribute if staying puts the commitment at risk.$qb$, $qb$Stay because the organizer requested it and work faster afterward.$qb$, $qb$Leave without explanation when the scheduled meeting time ends.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to finish your deadline work.$qb$, $qb$Stay and submit the afternoon work late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-132$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex analysis while team members regularly ask quick questions that interrupt your thinking. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set predictable times for routine questions, provide an urgent escalation route, and protect focused analysis periods so accessibility and concentration are both maintained.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team not to interrupt you until the analysis is complete.$qb$, $qb$Answer every question immediately to remain supportive.$qb$, $qb$Work on the analysis at night.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to send all questions to another colleague.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-153$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Last quarter, a predictable month-end surge caused backlogs and customer delays. The next surge is three weeks away and your team has the same staffing level. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the last surge to identify where capacity failed, estimate the upcoming demand, arrange preventive actions and backup support, and define indicators that will trigger extra capacity before service falls.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to prepare for a busy period and schedule additional progress checks during the final week.$qb$, $qb$Create an overtime roster now so additional hours are available if the backlog starts growing.$qb$, $qb$Wait until volumes rise before reallocating work because the forecast may be wrong.$qb$, $qb$Assume the team will handle the surge better this time because they now know what to expect.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-154$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You believe a customer issue is fixed, but the customer has not confirmed the result and the ticket deadline is today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to verify the result, document the solution and any remaining action, and close only when the agreed closure or no-response rule is met.$qb$, $qb$Send the solution and close the ticket, telling the customer they can reopen it if the issue continues.$qb$, $qb$Leave the ticket open until the customer replies, regardless of how long that takes.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether the ticket can be closed because the technical work is complete.$qb$, $qb$Close the ticket without another message because the fix has already been implemented.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-155$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says to obtain 'manager approval' but does not specify which manager. Different teams are now using different approvers. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended approval authority with the process owner, update the authoritative procedure with the exact role and exceptions, and communicate the clarified version.$qb$, $qb$Ask your own manager which approval they think is appropriate and use that interpretation within your team.$qb$, $qb$Add an example showing the approver your team normally uses, without changing the main wording.$qb$, $qb$Tell teams to use their local manager until the process owner updates the procedure.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording unchanged because flexibility allows teams to apply the rule sensibly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-156$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Thirty minutes before an important deadline, a colleague invites you to a useful but non-urgent discussion. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that you are protecting the deadline, ask for notes or a later time, and join only if the colleague identifies an issue that genuinely cannot wait.$qb$, $qb$Decline and tell the colleague you will reconnect after the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Join for ten minutes so you can contribute without losing too much working time.$qb$, $qb$Join the full discussion because collaboration should take priority over an individual deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the invitation without replying so you can stay focused.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-176$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Notifications keep interrupting you while you are preparing an analysis that requires sustained concentration. Most messages are not urgent, but colleagues expect reasonable responsiveness. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a protected focus period, mute non-urgent alerts, tell colleagues when you will check messages, and keep a route open for genuinely urgent issues.$qb$, $qb$Mute notifications for an hour and check all messages immediately afterward.$qb$, $qb$Leave alerts on but answer only messages that look quick to handle.$qb$, $qb$Do the analysis after normal hours when fewer people are messaging.$qb$, $qb$Respond whenever a message arrives so colleagues are never kept waiting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-200$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has delivered its main output, but two small defects and one documentation action remain. The team wants to mark it complete. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree which defects must be resolved before closure, assign the documentation action, confirm acceptance criteria, and close only when residual work is completed or formally transferred.$qb$, $qb$Mark the project complete but keep the three actions on a separate follow-up list.$qb$, $qb$Fix the two defects and leave the documentation to the operational team.$qb$, $qb$Keep the whole project open indefinitely until every minor issue disappears.$qb$, $qb$Close it now because the main output has already been delivered.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-202$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have promised two pieces of work by end of day. At noon, an unplanned task arrives that is important but can be completed tomorrow morning. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect today's commitments, tell the requester when you can start the new task, and schedule it for tomorrow morning unless its impact justifies explicit reprioritization.$qb$, $qb$Begin the new task after whichever existing commitment you finish first.$qb$, $qb$Work on all three during the afternoon so each makes progress.$qb$, $qb$Move one existing commitment to tomorrow and tell its recipient later.$qb$, $qb$Reject the new task because it was not part of today's plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-222$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are halfway through a detailed task when you notice you have opened email, chat, and several unrelated tabs without intending to. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Close the unrelated windows, define a focused work interval, note anything you genuinely need to revisit, and schedule a specific time to check messages afterward.$qb$, $qb$Close email and chat but leave the unrelated tabs open in case you need them.$qb$, $qb$Finish answering current messages so you can return with a clear inbox.$qb$, $qb$Move the detailed work to later in the day when you expect fewer interruptions.$qb$, $qb$Continue switching because checking each item takes only a few seconds.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-246$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague says a task is finished because the file has been delivered, but the recipient has reported one unresolved question that affects how they will use it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve or formally assign the question, confirm the recipient can use the deliverable as intended, and close the task only when the remaining issue has clear ownership.$qb$, $qb$Mark the task complete and answer the question separately when time allows.$qb$, $qb$Leave the task open but take no further action until the recipient follows up again.$qb$, $qb$Ask the recipient to close the task if the file itself is usable.$qb$, $qb$Close the task because delivery, not recipient interpretation, was the original commitment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-248$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing an important deliverable when a colleague asks for help on a problem that is urgent for them but can be solved by two other available people. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the urgency, direct them to an available capable colleague, and protect your deliverable while remaining available if the issue cannot be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Stop your work and help because the colleague describes the problem as urgent.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you cannot help and leave them to find another person.$qb$, $qb$Try to help through chat while continuing your deliverable at the same time.$qb$, $qb$Delay your deliverable without telling its recipient because helping a colleague is important.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-268$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have blocked time for complex work, but you keep remembering small unrelated tasks and stopping to handle them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Capture each unrelated task on a quick list without doing it, return to the complex work, and process the list at a planned break unless something is genuinely urgent.$qb$, $qb$Complete each small task immediately so it stops occupying your mind.$qb$, $qb$Try harder to ignore the thoughts without writing anything down.$qb$, $qb$End the focus block and clear all small tasks before restarting.$qb$, $qb$Switch between the complex work and small tasks whenever you remember them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-291$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring service contract renews in six weeks. Last year's renewal was delayed because legal review started too late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from the renewal date, engage legal and other dependencies now, identify required inputs and decision dates, and track early warning points for slippage.$qb$, $qb$Send the contract to legal four weeks before renewal instead of two weeks as last year.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to extend the current contract automatically if review runs late.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the vendor's renewal proposal before involving legal.$qb$, $qb$Assume the process will be faster this year because the contract is familiar.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-292$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A meeting ends with five agreed actions, but two actions have no named owner. Everyone is about to leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm an owner and due date for each unassigned action before closing the meeting and circulate the final action record.$qb$, $qb$List the two actions as 'team actions' and ask for volunteers by email later.$qb$, $qb$Take both actions yourself so nothing is lost.$qb$, $qb$Send the minutes and ask the manager to assign the missing owners.$qb$, $qb$Leave the actions unassigned because the responsible people will recognize what belongs to them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-294$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have a 30-minute meeting with four decisions to make. After 20 minutes, the group is still debating the first decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Call out the time remaining, determine whether the first decision can be closed or assigned for follow-up, and reprioritize the remaining agenda around the decisions that truly need the group.$qb$, $qb$Extend the meeting until all four decisions are completed.$qb$, $qb$End discussion on the first decision immediately and move through the remaining items quickly.$qb$, $qb$Let the first discussion continue because it is clearly important and drop the other items.$qb$, $qb$End the meeting on time and send all undecided items by email without assigning follow-up.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-314$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a complex document when a colleague starts an interesting but non-urgent conversation beside you. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague you want to continue the conversation later, note a suitable time if needed, and return your attention to the complex document.$qb$, $qb$Continue talking for a few minutes and then return to work.$qb$, $qb$Keep working while listening and responding to the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Move to another location without explaining why.$qb$, $qb$Stop the document because maintaining good colleague relationships is more important than a short period of focus.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-337$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major internal review is eight weeks away, and three teams must provide data in different formats. Last time, reconciliation consumed the final week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree common definitions and submission formats now, set staged data dates, test reconciliation early, and leave time to resolve discrepancies before the review.$qb$, $qb$Ask all teams to submit data one week earlier than last time.$qb$, $qb$Assign extra people to reconciliation during the final week.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the first submissions arrive before deciding whether formats need alignment.$qb$, $qb$Use whichever team's format is easiest and convert the others at the end.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-338$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You finish a task and hand it to another team, but they have not confirmed they understand the one remaining risk they now own. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the receiving team understands the risk, its trigger and required action, record the ownership, and close your task only after the handover is explicit.$qb$, $qb$Send another email describing the risk and close the task.$qb$, $qb$Keep the task open until the risk itself disappears.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to confirm that the receiving team now owns the risk.$qb$, $qb$Close the task because the handover email already mentioned the risk.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-340$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are scheduled for two back-to-back meetings. The first meeting has reached an important topic just as it should end, and the second meeting needs you to make a decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State that you must leave, agree an owner or follow-up for the unfinished topic, and join the second meeting on time so its decision is not delayed.$qb$, $qb$Stay five more minutes in the first meeting and join the second late.$qb$, $qb$Leave immediately without arranging follow-up because the second meeting is more important.$qb$, $qb$Ask the second meeting to start without you and wait until the first topic is resolved.$qb$, $qb$Stay in the first meeting because leaving during an important topic would be disrespectful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-360$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are writing a detailed proposal when you notice that every new email notification makes you read the subject line even if you do not reply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Turn off visible notifications for a defined focus period, set a planned email-check time, and preserve an urgent-contact route if your role requires one.$qb$, $qb$Keep notifications on but train yourself not to read the subject lines.$qb$, $qb$Check each email quickly so it stops distracting you.$qb$, $qb$Move the proposal work to the evening when fewer emails arrive.$qb$, $qb$Accept the distraction because reading a subject line takes very little time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-383$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project depends on a regulatory approval that normally takes four weeks but sometimes takes eight. The target launch is ten weeks away. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Submit as early as possible, plan around the longer credible approval window, identify work that can proceed safely in parallel, and define the latest decision point for protecting the launch.$qb$, $qb$Plan for the normal four-week approval and add one week of contingency.$qb$, $qb$Ask the regulator to guarantee the four-week timeline.$qb$, $qb$Wait until all project work is complete before submitting the approval package.$qb$, $qb$Keep the launch date unchanged and solve any approval delay if it occurs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-384$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring problem has been fixed and no incidents have occurred for a week, but the temporary workaround is still active. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the permanent fix is stable against the agreed observation period, remove the temporary workaround safely, update documentation, and close the incident only after normal operation is restored.$qb$, $qb$Close the incident now and remove the workaround when someone has time.$qb$, $qb$Keep the workaround permanently because it provides extra protection.$qb$, $qb$Remove the workaround immediately because no incidents occurred this week.$qb$, $qb$Leave the incident open indefinitely in case the problem returns.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-386$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have one hour reserved for a task requiring concentration. A colleague asks for a 20-minute discussion that can happen any time today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the focus hour and offer a specific later time for the discussion unless the colleague identifies a reason it cannot wait.$qb$, $qb$Take the discussion now and extend the focus task by 20 minutes.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague you are busy and ask them to try again later.$qb$, $qb$Discuss the issue through chat while continuing the focus task.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the focus block because collaborative requests should take priority.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-406$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are reading a complex document when incoming calendar reminders repeatedly pull your attention away even though none require immediate action. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Silence non-essential reminders for the reading block, note the appointments you genuinely need to remember, and restore alerts when the focused work ends.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss each reminder as it appears and continue reading.$qb$, $qb$Leave the reminders visible so you do not miss anything important.$qb$, $qb$Stop reading and prepare for all upcoming appointments first.$qb$, $qb$Move the complex reading to a time when your calendar is empty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-429$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical software license expires in three months. Renewal requires budget approval, security review, and vendor paperwork from different teams. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from expiry, identify each dependency and lead time, start the longest steps early, and set decision points that preserve continuity if one approval slips.$qb$, $qb$Submit the budget request now and start the other steps after funding is approved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to remind you one month before expiry.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the final month because renewals are usually routine.$qb$, $qb$Assume temporary license extensions will be available if approval is late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-430$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have resolved a recurring issue and documented the fix, but one temporary access permission granted during troubleshooting is still active. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or formally review the temporary access, confirm normal controls are restored, and close the issue only after all temporary recovery measures are accounted for.$qb$, $qb$Close the issue and ask the access team to remove the permission later.$qb$, $qb$Keep the permission because it may be useful if the issue returns.$qb$, $qb$Remove the permission immediately without checking whether any recovery work still depends on it.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the access because it was not the cause of the original issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-432$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to review a document today. They did not give a deadline, and you already have committed work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask when the review is actually needed, explain your existing commitments, and agree a realistic review time based on the document's impact and urgency.$qb$, $qb$Review it immediately because the colleague asked today.$qb$, $qb$Put it at the end of your task list and review it if time remains.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague you are too busy and ask them to find someone else.$qb$, $qb$Assume end of day is the deadline and work later if necessary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-452$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a difficult task when you notice yourself repeatedly checking progress metrics that change only once a day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove the metric from immediate view, set a sensible time to check it, and return attention to the task whose progress you can actually influence now.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric open but try to check it less often.$qb$, $qb$Check it one final time and then close it.$qb$, $qb$Pause the difficult task until the metric updates so you know whether your work is still needed.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because staying informed helps motivation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-475$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project needs specialist testing in six weeks, and the specialist team is already heavily booked. Your exact test date is not yet fixed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Engage the specialist team now, reserve a realistic window or agree booking triggers, and plan upstream work so the final test date can be confirmed before capacity disappears.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the exact date is known before making a booking.$qb$, $qb$Book the earliest available date even if your project may not be ready.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project sponsor to secure priority access to the specialists.$qb$, $qb$Assume the specialist team will fit the work in because the project is important.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-476$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A supplier issue is resolved and the replacement item has arrived, but the original faulty item is still recorded as active inventory. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Complete the inventory correction or return process, confirm the replacement is accepted, and close the issue only when both physical and system records match reality.$qb$, $qb$Close the issue because the replacement has arrived and fix inventory later.$qb$, $qb$Remove the faulty item from the system without completing the return process.$qb$, $qb$Keep both items active until the next inventory count.$qb$, $qb$Ask inventory staff to handle it and close your issue immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-478$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A task due tomorrow could be made noticeably better with another half-day of work, but it already meets the agreed requirement. You also have another commitment due tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop improving the first task once it meets the required standard, protect the second commitment, and note optional enhancements for later if they create enough value.$qb$, $qb$Spend the extra half-day improving the first task because quality matters.$qb$, $qb$Split the remaining time equally between improving the first task and completing the second.$qb$, $qb$Ask for an extension on the second task so you can perfect the first.$qb$, $qb$Continue polishing until you personally feel satisfied with the quality.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-498$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex calculation when colleagues nearby begin discussing a topic that interests you but does not involve your work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the pull on your attention, reduce the distraction by moving or using an appropriate focus method, and return to the calculation without joining the discussion.$qb$, $qb$Listen while continuing the calculation because you do not need to participate.$qb$, $qb$Pause the calculation briefly to hear the interesting part.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleagues to stop talking because you need concentration.$qb$, $qb$Continue working in the same environment and accept that some distraction is unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-521$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key external expert will be available for only one week next month, but several project decisions may need their input. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the decisions that truly require the expert, prepare the necessary evidence beforehand, reserve time during their availability, and define alternatives for issues that arise later.$qb$, $qb$Book the expert for the entire week so they are available whenever needed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until questions arise and contact the expert during their available week.$qb$, $qb$Ask the project sponsor to extend the expert's availability.$qb$, $qb$Assume internal staff can handle anything that comes up after the expert leaves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-522$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer has accepted a corrected deliverable, but an internal lesson-learned action from the error has not been assigned. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign and date the preventive action, record who owns follow-up, and close the customer issue while keeping the improvement action visible in the appropriate tracking process.$qb$, $qb$Keep the customer issue open until the lesson-learned action is completed.$qb$, $qb$Close everything because the customer has accepted the correction.$qb$, $qb$Take the improvement action yourself so the issue can close cleanly.$qb$, $qb$Add the lesson to meeting notes without assigning an owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-524$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are halfway through a planned task when a meeting invitation arrives for later today. The meeting is optional and the agenda shows no item requiring your input. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline or ask for notes, protect the planned task, and attend only if the organizer identifies a specific need for your contribution.$qb$, $qb$Accept because attending helps you stay informed.$qb$, $qb$Join for the first ten minutes and leave if nothing relevant appears.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the invitation without responding.$qb$, $qb$Move the planned task to tomorrow because meetings are harder to reschedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-544$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are reviewing a long document and keep switching to easy formatting changes instead of addressing a difficult analytical section. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Notice the avoidance pattern, define a focused block for the difficult section, park cosmetic edits on a list, and complete the high-value thinking before returning to formatting.$qb$, $qb$Finish the formatting first so the document feels more organized.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between analysis and formatting to maintain motivation.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to handle the difficult section.$qb$, $qb$Continue with whichever part feels easiest because all work eventually needs completion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-567$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A product launch in two months depends on packaging that has a six-week supplier lead time. The final design is still being refined. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which design elements must freeze for ordering, set the latest safe decision date, explore provisional or staged ordering, and protect a fallback if design changes continue.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final design so the supplier receives one correct order.$qb$, $qb$Order the current design now and accept rework if it changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the supplier to reduce the lead time once the design is final.$qb$, $qb$Keep the launch date and let the packaging team solve any late design impact.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-568$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project action is technically complete, but the evidence required for audit has not been uploaded to the agreed repository. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Upload or obtain the required evidence, verify it is linked to the action, and mark the action complete only when both work and required proof are present.$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete and upload the evidence later.$qb$, $qb$Leave it open but do not chase the evidence because the work itself is finished.$qb$, $qb$Ask audit staff to collect the evidence if they need it.$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete because audit documentation is separate from delivery.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-570$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are close to finishing a task when a low-priority meeting begins. Leaving now would allow you to finish in ten minutes, but the meeting organizer expects attendance. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check whether your presence is actually needed for any agenda item, communicate promptly, and if not, decline or join later rather than sacrificing focused completion solely from habit.$qb$, $qb$Join the meeting on time and finish the task afterward.$qb$, $qb$Skip the meeting without telling anyone and finish the task.$qb$, $qb$Join while continuing to work on the task in the background.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to move the whole meeting by ten minutes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-590$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on an important task when you notice you are repeatedly refreshing a dashboard for an unrelated issue that someone else owns. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm whether you have any action on the unrelated issue, stop monitoring it if not, and return attention to your task with a planned point to check only if needed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the dashboard open but try to refresh it less often.$qb$, $qb$Ask the issue owner to message you whenever anything changes.$qb$, $qb$Pause your important task until the unrelated issue is resolved.$qb$, $qb$Continue monitoring because staying informed may help if support is later needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-613$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly audit requires evidence from several teams. Last year, some evidence could not be found because owners had left the company. The next audit is four months away. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the required evidence and durable storage locations now, assign current owners, test retrieval before the audit, and close gaps while knowledgeable people are still available.$qb$, $qb$Ask teams to start collecting evidence one month before the audit.$qb$, $qb$Create a list of last year's missing evidence and focus only on those items.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to keep copies of important evidence personally.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the auditor's request list because requirements may change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-614$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A completed project has one temporary spreadsheet still being used because the permanent system update was delayed. The project team is about to disband. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign ownership of the temporary tool, document its limits and retirement trigger, and transfer the permanent-system action before closing the project team.$qb$, $qb$Keep one project member assigned informally until the system update happens.$qb$, $qb$Close the project and let operations manage the spreadsheet.$qb$, $qb$Stop using the spreadsheet immediately because temporary tools should not survive project closure.$qb$, $qb$Leave the spreadsheet without an owner because everyone knows it is temporary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-616$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague sends you a draft for review two hours before their deadline, but you already have a higher-impact commitment due at the same time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague what review you can realistically provide, protect the higher-impact commitment, and help them find another reviewer or narrow the review if their deadline cannot move.$qb$, $qb$Stop your own work and review the draft because their deadline is sooner.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the draft until your commitment is finished.$qb$, $qb$Review only the first half without telling the colleague the rest was not checked.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager which person's deadline matters more.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-636$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult analysis when you notice you are repeatedly reorganizing your notes instead of making the key judgment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the low-value organizing as avoidance, define the decision question, work on the evidence needed for that judgment, and postpone cosmetic organization until afterward.$qb$, $qb$Finish organizing the notes so the analysis becomes easier.$qb$, $qb$Switch to another task until you feel ready to make the judgment.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to make the key judgment from your notes.$qb$, $qb$Continue organizing because good structure is essential to analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-659$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A quarterly review needs input from a specialist who will be on leave during the final preparation week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify the specialist's required contribution now, obtain or schedule it before their leave, document assumptions, and arrange a backup for questions that arise later.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to remain reachable for urgent questions while on leave.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the week before the review and use another colleague if needed.$qb$, $qb$Move the entire review until the specialist returns.$qb$, $qb$Assume the specialist's previous quarter input will still be sufficient.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-662$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have planned an hour for an important analysis. A colleague asks for help with a task that is important but not due until tomorrow afternoon. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the analysis block and agree a specific later time to help, unless the colleague identifies a dependency that makes waiting genuinely costly.$qb$, $qb$Help immediately because the colleague's task is important.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you cannot help today.$qb$, $qb$Work on their task and your analysis at the same time.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else without checking whether you can help later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-682$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a complex task when you notice you are repeatedly checking whether colleagues have replied to messages that are not needed until tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later time to check those replies, close the messaging view, and return attention to the complex task unless a response becomes genuinely time-critical.$qb$, $qb$Keep the messages visible but try not to check them.$qb$, $qb$Send follow-up messages so colleagues reply sooner.$qb$, $qb$Pause the complex task until all replies arrive.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because waiting for responses is part of coordination.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-705$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A major customer review is scheduled in ten weeks, but the data needed for one key metric is collected only monthly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Work backward from the review, identify the last usable collection point, validate the metric early, and create a contingency if the final monthly data is late or incomplete.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final monthly collection because it will be the most current.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous month's data and update it on the day of the review.$qb$, $qb$Ask the data team to change its collection frequency for this review.$qb$, $qb$Remove the metric if the final data is not ready.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-706$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has handed over a new process, but the receiving team has not yet confirmed that the first live case worked as expected. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Agree a short post-handover validation point, confirm the first live case or defined acceptance evidence, and close the transition only when operational use is demonstrated.$qb$, $qb$Close the project because the process and documentation were handed over.$qb$, $qb$Keep the project open for several months in case problems emerge.$qb$, $qb$Ask the receiving team to contact you if the first case fails.$qb$, $qb$Run the first live case yourself even though the receiving team now owns the process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-708$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A meeting you attend every week rarely needs your input, but occasionally an important issue for your work appears without warning. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the pattern with the organizer, arrange agenda or tagging rules that alert you when your input is needed, and stop attending routinely if reliable visibility can be maintained.$qb$, $qb$Keep attending because an important issue may appear.$qb$, $qb$Stop attending and ask colleagues to tell you afterward if anything matters.$qb$, $qb$Join every meeting but work on other tasks in the background.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to send you full minutes after each meeting and decide later whether you should have attended.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-728$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are doing deep work when you notice that you keep opening a news site during short pauses without intending to. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or block the easy distraction for the focus period, choose a deliberate break activity, and return to the task rather than relying only on repeated self-control.$qb$, $qb$Close the site each time you notice it.$qb$, $qb$Allow a few minutes of browsing because short breaks improve focus.$qb$, $qb$Move the work to another time when you feel more disciplined.$qb$, $qb$Keep the site open so checking it becomes less tempting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-751$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A specialist vendor contract ends in five months, but replacing the vendor would require three months of qualification and testing. Performance is currently acceptable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Start renewal and alternative-market review early, define the latest decision date that preserves switching time, and gather performance evidence before the qualification window closes.$qb$, $qb$Wait until two months before expiry because current performance is acceptable.$qb$, $qb$Begin full qualification of a replacement immediately.$qb$, $qb$Assume the current vendor will renew on similar terms.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor for an automatic extension if negotiations run late.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-752$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has completed an improvement and the new process is live, but one old instruction page is still accessible and may confuse users. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove, archive or redirect the obsolete instruction, confirm users reach the current guidance, and close the change only when the old route no longer creates ambiguity.$qb$, $qb$Close the improvement and update the old page at the next documentation review.$qb$, $qb$Add a note to the old page saying a newer process exists.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team not to use the old page.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the new process is already live.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-754$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are invited to a meeting with a broad agenda. Only the final ten minutes concern your work, and you have an important deadline that day. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask whether you can join for the relevant section or provide input asynchronously, and protect the rest of the time for the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Attend the full meeting because leaving early may appear disrespectful.$qb$, $qb$Decline the meeting entirely and ask for minutes.$qb$, $qb$Join the full meeting but work on your deadline in the background.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to move your topic to the start for your convenience.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-774$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a demanding task when you notice yourself checking a shared chat for reactions to a message you posted earlier. No response is needed today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later check time, close the chat during the focus block, and return to the demanding task rather than repeatedly seeking non-urgent social feedback.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat open but stop refreshing it manually.$qb$, $qb$Send another message to encourage replies sooner.$qb$, $qb$Pause the demanding task until someone responds.$qb$, $qb$Continue checking because knowing colleagues' reactions may affect your work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-797$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly license renewal requires a usage forecast, but the person who prepares that forecast will leave the company two months before renewal. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Capture the forecasting method and assumptions before they leave, assign a successor, run a practice forecast, and set renewal milestones so the dependency is transferred early.$qb$, $qb$Ask the person to prepare the renewal forecast before leaving even though it will be old.$qb$, $qb$Wait until renewal and ask finance to estimate usage.$qb$, $qb$Ask the departing employee to remain available after leaving.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's forecast with a growth adjustment.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-798$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A defect is fixed and tested, but the customer-facing team still has an old workaround in its script. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Update the script, tell the customer-facing team the workaround is retired, and close the defect only after normal guidance reflects the permanent fix.$qb$, $qb$Close the defect because the technical fix passed testing.$qb$, $qb$Leave the workaround in the script as a backup.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer-facing team to update the script when convenient.$qb$, $qb$Remove the script entirely until someone rewrites it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-800$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for a quick call while you are finishing work due in 25 minutes. Their issue is important but has no immediate deadline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that you are protecting the imminent deadline and offer a specific time shortly afterward, unless they identify an impact that makes the call genuinely urgent.$qb$, $qb$Take the call because they described the issue as important.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the message until your work is submitted.$qb$, $qb$Call while continuing to finish your work.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else even though you can help later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-820$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are analyzing a difficult issue when you notice you keep opening the task list to check easier work you could finish quickly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the pull toward easy completion, set a bounded period for the difficult analysis, hide the task list temporarily, and return to easier work afterward.$qb$, $qb$Complete a few easy tasks first to build momentum.$qb$, $qb$Keep the task list open but promise yourself not to switch.$qb$, $qb$Move the difficult issue to tomorrow when you have more energy.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to analyze the difficult issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-843$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical project workshop is in seven weeks, and two decision-makers have conflicting travel schedules. Their joint decision is needed during the workshop. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve availability early, identify alternate dates or decision methods, and secure a workable decision window before other workshop dependencies become fixed.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the workshop is closer because travel schedules may change.$qb$, $qb$Invite both and proceed with whoever can attend.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to delegate the decision without discussing it first.$qb$, $qb$Move the workshop online automatically.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-844$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process change is live and working, but a temporary daily monitoring report is still being produced even though its agreed monitoring period ended. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the monitoring results, confirm whether the temporary control can be retired, stop it if exit criteria are met, and document the transition to normal monitoring.$qb$, $qb$Keep the report because extra monitoring cannot hurt.$qb$, $qb$Stop it immediately because the planned period ended.$qb$, $qb$Continue until someone complains about the effort.$qb$, $qb$Ask the report producer to decide whether it is still useful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-846$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have 45 minutes before a fixed deadline. A teammate asks you to proofread a non-urgent document that will be sent tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the fixed deadline and agree a review time afterward or the next morning, unless the teammate identifies a reason the document cannot wait.$qb$, $qb$Proofread quickly because helping a teammate should take only a few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Proofread while continuing your own work.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else even though you can help later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-866$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are drafting an important recommendation and keep switching to formatting the slides because it feels easier than resolving one uncertain conclusion. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Park the formatting, define what evidence or judgment the uncertain conclusion needs, and spend a focused block resolving that issue before returning to presentation work.$qb$, $qb$Finish formatting first so the deck is ready.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between formatting and analysis to avoid getting stuck.$qb$, $qb$Remove the uncertain conclusion from the presentation.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to decide the conclusion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-890$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A temporary manual reconciliation introduced during a system outage is still running three weeks after the system recovered. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the monitoring results, confirm exit criteria are met, retire the temporary report, and document the transition to normal monitoring.$qb$, $qb$Keep the report because extra monitoring cannot hurt.$qb$, $qb$Stop it immediately only because the planned period ended.$qb$, $qb$Continue until someone complains about the effort.$qb$, $qb$Ask the report producer to decide.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-892$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have reserved the final hour of the day to submit a regulatory response. A colleague invites you to an optional brainstorming session at the same time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the fixed deadline and agree a review time afterward or next morning, unless they identify an impact that makes the document genuinely urgent.$qb$, $qb$Proofread quickly because it should take only a few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Proofread while continuing your own work.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else even though you can help later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-935$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key insurance certificate must be renewed before a customer audit, but the broker who normally arranges it is changing firms next month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Capture the evidence process, assign and train a successor, and run a retrieval check before the employee transfers.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to prepare the whole submission now.$qb$, $qb$Wait until renewal time.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to remain available after transfer.$qb$, $qb$Reuse last year's package.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-938$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are due to submit a tender response in 35 minutes when a colleague asks you to join an informal discussion about next month's office event. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the regulatory deadline and decline or provide input asynchronously unless the brainstorming genuinely requires you.$qb$, $qb$Attend because collaboration matters.$qb$, $qb$Join and work on the response simultaneously.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to move the session.$qb$, $qb$Delay the response.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-958$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$While preparing a sensitive budget analysis, you keep reorganizing folders and filenames instead of resolving the main assumption.$qb$, $qb$Recognize the avoidance, define the assumption to resolve, block time for the analysis, and postpone file organization.$qb$, $qb$Finish organizing first.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between both tasks.$qb$, $qb$Remove the assumption.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to decide.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-981$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key annual dataset will change owners six weeks before the reporting cycle.$qb$, $qb$Transfer definitions, extraction steps and access early, run a trial retrieval, and confirm the timetable before ownership changes.$qb$, $qb$Ask the current owner to prepare everything early.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the new owner to learn it.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's data.$qb$, $qb$Contact the former owner later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-984$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A regulatory response is due at noon while an optional recorded town hall starts at 10:30.$qb$, $qb$Use the recording later and protect the deadline unless your live participation is specifically required.$qb$, $qb$Attend because town halls matter.$qb$, $qb$Join while working on the response.$qb$, $qb$Ask for a deadline extension.$qb$, $qb$Skip without checking attendance requirements.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2817$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A critical renewal requires three approvals. One approver will be on extended leave during the normal approval week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Plan the approval sequence early, obtain that approver's input before leave or arrange the authorized delegate, and protect the renewal deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ask the approver to check email while on leave.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the normal approval week and then escalate.$qb$, $qb$Remove that approval because the person is unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Submit the renewal late if necessary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2818$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have a fixed customer call in 20 minutes and are asked to join an internal discussion that may last an hour. Your input is useful but not essential. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain the fixed commitment, provide any useful input now or asynchronously, and protect the customer call rather than joining a discussion you cannot complete.$qb$, $qb$Join the discussion and leave without warning when the customer call starts.$qb$, $qb$Move the customer call because the internal discussion started first.$qb$, $qb$Decline the discussion without offering any input.$qb$, $qb$Join both calls at the same time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2834$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult analysis and notice yourself repeatedly replying to non-urgent messages because each reply feels easy to finish. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a communication check time, close non-urgent messaging during a focused block, and return to the analysis until the planned break.$qb$, $qb$Keep messaging open but try to reply less.$qb$, $qb$Finish all easy messages first to clear your mind.$qb$, $qb$Move the analysis to tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to handle the analysis.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1119$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key supplier review is in three months, but the performance analyst who prepares the scorecard is moving to another role next month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Transfer the scorecard method, data access and definitions to a new owner, run a trial update before the move, and set the review timetable early.$qb$, $qb$Ask the analyst to prepare the scorecard three months early.$qb$, $qb$Wait for their replacement to learn the process.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous review's scorecard.$qb$, $qb$Ask the analyst to continue preparing it informally after moving roles.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1122$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are due to present to a customer in 25 minutes when a colleague asks you to review a document that is not due until tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer preparation, tell the colleague when you can review the document afterward, and check whether any genuine dependency makes it more urgent than stated.$qb$, $qb$Review it now because helping colleagues is important.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Review it while preparing your customer presentation.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to find someone else without offering later help.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1142$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult recommendation and keep checking email for a response that is useful but not required until tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later time to check for the response, close email during a focus block, and progress the recommendation using what can be done now.$qb$, $qb$Keep email open so you see the response immediately.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated follow-ups to get the response sooner.$qb$, $qb$Stop the recommendation until the response arrives.$qb$, $qb$Switch to easier tasks for the rest of the day.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1165$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A quarterly customer review requires survey results, but the survey platform contract expires two weeks before the review. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Plan the final survey and export timing, preserve the required data in an approved location, and test access before the platform contract ends.$qb$, $qb$Renew the platform solely for the review.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the review and ask the vendor for temporary access.$qb$, $qb$Use screenshots of the survey dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Drop survey results from the review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1168$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a time-sensitive analysis when a meeting invitation arrives for the same hour. The agenda contains one item where your input would be helpful but can be provided in writing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send your input in advance, decline or attend only the relevant portion if needed, and protect the analysis deadline.$qb$, $qb$Attend the full meeting because your input is helpful.$qb$, $qb$Decline without providing input.$qb$, $qb$Join the meeting while doing the analysis.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to reschedule the entire meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1188$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are trying to solve a complex problem but keep checking a live metric that changes every few minutes even though the next decision is not due for two hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a meaningful next check time or threshold, close the metric view, and focus on the analysis that can change the upcoming decision.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric visible so you never miss a change.$qb$, $qb$Refresh it less often but leave it open.$qb$, $qb$Stop the analysis until the metric stabilizes.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to watch the metric continuously.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1211$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A mandatory certification expires in six months, but the training provider has announced a three-month closure during the normal renewal period.$qb$, $qb$Bring renewal forward or secure an approved alternative provider, plan employee availability, and verify completion before the closure creates a compliance gap.$qb$, $qb$Wait because six months is still a long time.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to renew during the provider closure if needed.$qb$, $qb$Let certifications lapse briefly because the provider is unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Remove certified work from employees only after expiry.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1214$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a deadline when a colleague asks for help preparing a meeting that is three days away. They say it will only take ten minutes.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current deadline, agree a specific later time to help, and switch only if they identify a genuine dependency that cannot wait.$qb$, $qb$Help now because it is only ten minutes.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until your deadline is complete.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else permanently.$qb$, $qb$Help while continuing your own work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1234$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex model and repeatedly checking whether a colleague has replied to a non-blocking question.$qb$, $qb$Record the assumption you can use temporarily, set a later check time, and continue the model rather than repeatedly interrupting focus for a non-blocking response.$qb$, $qb$Keep checking because the answer may improve the model.$qb$, $qb$Stop work until the reply arrives.$qb$, $qb$Send repeated follow-ups.$qb$, $qb$Switch to unrelated easy work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1257$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A yearly customer certification needs signatures from two executives. One executive's role is being replaced during the certification month. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify who will hold signing authority, secure the transition and timetable early, and ensure the new or outgoing executive can complete the requirement legitimately.$qb$, $qb$Ask the outgoing executive to sign the future certification before leaving.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the new executive starts.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's signatures.$qb$, $qb$Submit with one signature and explain later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1260$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have reserved an hour for a complex customer proposal when a colleague asks you to join an internal meeting whose decisions can be reviewed afterward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the proposal block, tell the organizer you will review the decisions afterward, and join only if your live input becomes necessary.$qb$, $qb$Attend because internal decisions may affect you.$qb$, $qb$Join and work on the proposal during the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Decline and ignore the meeting outcome.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to postpone the meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1280$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a complex forecast and keep checking a team chat where colleagues are discussing an unrelated social event. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Mute or close the chat for a defined focus period, complete the forecast work that needs concentration, and check the social discussion later.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat open but try not to read it.$qb$, $qb$Join the discussion briefly whenever a new message appears.$qb$, $qb$Move the forecast to another day.$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues to stop chatting until you finish.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1303$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A regulatory filing is due in five months, and the software used to generate one required report will be replaced in three months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required report definition, preserve or recreate the reporting capability in the new system, and test the output before the old software is retired.$qb$, $qb$Generate the filing report five months early.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the new system and assume it will contain the report.$qb$, $qb$Ask IT to keep the old software indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Remove the report if the new system cannot produce it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1306$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are due to submit an important analysis in an hour. A colleague asks you to attend a routine meeting where the agenda shows no item needing your decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline or use the meeting record later, protect the analysis time, and tell the organizer you are available if an unexpected decision genuinely needs you.$qb$, $qb$Attend because routine meetings are commitments.$qb$, $qb$Join and work on the analysis in parallel.$qb$, $qb$Ask the organizer to cancel the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Skip without telling anyone.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1326$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a complex document and repeatedly adjust fonts and spacing while one important argument remains unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the formatting as low-value avoidance, define the unresolved argument and evidence needed, and return to presentation polish only after the reasoning is settled.$qb$, $qb$Finish formatting first so the document feels complete.$qb$, $qb$Alternate formatting and reasoning every few minutes.$qb$, $qb$Remove the unresolved argument.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to write the difficult section.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1349$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A mandatory license renewal is due shortly after the employee who manages it transfers to another division.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the renewal calendar, evidence, access and ownership before the move, and run a readiness check with the new owner.$qb$, $qb$Ask the employee to renew it early regardless of the valid renewal window.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the new owner to discover the requirement.$qb$, $qb$Ask the former employee to handle it informally.$qb$, $qb$Let the license lapse briefly if needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1352$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a fixed deadline when invited to a routine meeting where your only item can be answered by email.$qb$, $qb$Send the answer in advance, protect the deadline, and attend only if live discussion becomes genuinely necessary.$qb$, $qb$Attend the full meeting because you are on the agenda.$qb$, $qb$Join while working on your deadline.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the meeting invitation.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to move the meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1372$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$While preparing a difficult analysis, you keep checking whether a low-priority task has been completed because it gives you a sense of progress.$qb$, $qb$Recognize the progress-seeking distraction, set a later check point, and protect a focused block for the difficult analysis.$qb$, $qb$Keep checking because monitoring tasks is part of your job.$qb$, $qb$Complete the low-priority task yourself.$qb$, $qb$Switch entirely to smaller tasks.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to monitor everything for you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1395$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer contract renewal is six months away, but the usage data needed for pricing will move to a new reporting platform in four months. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the pricing data and calculation now, ensure the new platform can reproduce it, and run a comparison before the old source is retired.$qb$, $qb$Export six months of current data now and use it later.$qb$, $qb$Wait until renewal because the new platform should be ready.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer to accept last year's usage basis.$qb$, $qb$Keep the old reporting platform active only for this contract.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1398$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have a fixed external deadline this afternoon. A colleague asks you to join a brainstorming session where ideas can also be added to a shared document. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Add any useful ideas asynchronously and protect the deadline, joining live only if your participation is essential to an immediate decision.$qb$, $qb$Attend because brainstorming works best live.$qb$, $qb$Decline and contribute nothing.$qb$, $qb$Join while finishing your deadline work.$qb$, $qb$Ask the group to postpone brainstorming.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1418$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are analyzing a difficult issue and keep checking a dashboard whose numbers will not be refreshed until the next hour. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Note the next refresh time, close the dashboard, and focus on analysis that can be completed with current information until the data actually changes.$qb$, $qb$Keep refreshing in case the data updates early.$qb$, $qb$Stop all analysis until the dashboard refreshes.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to watch the dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Switch to unrelated small tasks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1441$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key annual submission depends on data from a vendor portal whose contract ends before the submission date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the submission data now, arrange an approved export or replacement access before the vendor portal closes, and test retrieval.$qb$, $qb$Wait until submission time.$qb$, $qb$Download everything from the portal now without defining what is needed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the vendor to keep access open informally.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's submission data.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1444$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are finishing a fixed-deadline customer document when invited to a routine internal call where your input is optional. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Provide any useful input asynchronously and protect the fixed customer deadline, joining live only if an actual decision requires you.$qb$, $qb$Attend the call because it is already scheduled.$qb$, $qb$Join while finishing the customer document.$qb$, $qb$Skip without responding.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to move the call.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1464$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are drafting a complex recommendation and keep checking a non-urgent chat because replies arrive frequently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Mute the non-urgent chat for a defined focus block and return to it after resolving the core recommendation work.$qb$, $qb$Keep the chat open but try not to read it.$qb$, $qb$Reply quickly whenever a message appears.$qb$, $qb$Move the recommendation to another day.$qb$, $qb$Ask colleagues to stop chatting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1487$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring customer report depends on a specialist data extract, and that specialist will be unavailable during the next reporting cycle. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Transfer the extraction steps and access, have another person run a practice extract, and confirm definitions before the specialist becomes unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Ask the specialist to prepare the next report early.$qb$, $qb$Wait and contact them during their absence if needed.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous period's data.$qb$, $qb$Skip the specialist section for one cycle.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1490$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have blocked time to finish a proposal. A colleague sends several non-urgent questions that can wait until tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge receipt if needed, give a time when you will respond, and protect the focus block unless one question becomes genuinely time-critical.$qb$, $qb$Answer each question as it arrives.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the colleague completely until tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to find someone else.$qb$, $qb$Work on the proposal and questions simultaneously.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1510$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a difficult task and keep opening a completed report to make small wording improvements that are not due today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the easier polishing task as a distraction, close it, and protect a focused block for the difficult work before returning to optional improvements.$qb$, $qb$Finish polishing the report so it is fully done.$qb$, $qb$Keep both documents open and switch when stuck.$qb$, $qb$Move the difficult task to tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to polish the report while you work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1533$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer review next quarter needs data from an employee survey, but the survey owner is leaving before the survey closes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Transfer survey administration, access, definitions and reporting steps to a named owner before departure, and test that the new owner can retrieve the needed data.$qb$, $qb$Ask the departing owner to close the survey early.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the survey closes and ask HR for help.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's survey data.$qb$, $qb$Ask the former employee to assist after leaving.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1536$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You have set aside the morning to complete a tender response. A colleague asks you to sit in on a practice presentation this afternoon; they want feedback before lunch. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer preparation and agree a specific later review time, unless the colleague identifies a real dependency that makes the request urgent.$qb$, $qb$Review it now because it is quick.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the request until the due date.$qb$, $qb$Review it while preparing for the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to find someone else permanently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1556$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are working on a difficult analysis and keep checking a shared task board even though no relevant task is due to change today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set a later review time, close the board, and protect a focused block for the analysis rather than monitoring information that cannot affect the current work.$qb$, $qb$Keep the board open so you stay informed.$qb$, $qb$Refresh it less often but continue checking.$qb$, $qb$Stop the analysis until other tasks change.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to message you about every update.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1579$qb$, $qb$A7-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer contract requires an annual evidence pack, and the employee who knows where older evidence is stored plans to retire before the next pack is due. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Map the required evidence locations, transfer access and retrieval knowledge to a named owner, and run a sample retrieval before the employee leaves.$qb$, $qb$Ask the employee to prepare next year's pack early.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the annual pack is due.$qb$, $qb$Keep the employee's contact details for later questions.$qb$, $qb$Use only new evidence next year.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1582$qb$, $qb$A7-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are finalizing a time-sensitive customer response when a colleague asks you to review slides for a meeting next week. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer response and agree a later review slot that still gives the colleague enough time before next week's meeting.$qb$, $qb$Review the slides now because the request is quick.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the colleague until next week.$qb$, $qb$Review slides while writing the customer response.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to find another reviewer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1602$qb$, $qb$A7-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are preparing a difficult presentation and keep adjusting animations that add little value while the conclusion still needs stronger evidence. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the low-value formatting, identify the evidence needed for the conclusion, and return to presentation polish only after the reasoning is sound.$qb$, $qb$Finish the animations first so the deck is nearly complete.$qb$, $qb$Alternate between animations and evidence gathering.$qb$, $qb$Remove the conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to find the evidence while you format.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
