  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-361$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project schedule leaves no recovery time after three consecutive high-intensity milestones. The team says they can manage if necessary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review whether all milestone dates and scope are genuinely fixed, build realistic recovery or capacity relief into the plan, and escalate trade-offs before sustained overload becomes the operating assumption.$qb$, $qb$Keep the schedule but encourage everyone to take short breaks between milestones.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers who are willing to work the extra hours.$qb$, $qb$Reduce documentation and review work during the intense period.$qb$, $qb$Accept the plan because the team has said they can manage.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-385$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shared instruction says an exception requires 'senior approval' but does not define what level counts as senior. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended approving role with the policy owner, update the instruction with the exact role or authority threshold, and communicate the clarification to current users.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager who they consider senior enough and use that locally.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of people who have approved exceptions before.$qb$, $qb$Let each department interpret 'senior' according to its own structure.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording flexible because organizational titles change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-387$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A dashboard fails just before a review, but the underlying approved data export is available. The review needs only three key measures. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Calculate the three required measures from the approved export using known definitions, clearly label the temporary method, and reconcile the figures when the dashboard returns.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous dashboard values and explain that they may be slightly old.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the review because the normal dashboard is unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Ask another team for screenshots of their dashboard.$qb$, $qb$Use an online analytics tool to upload the export and recreate the dashboard quickly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-407$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are planning a project after a previous project succeeded only because several people worked most weekends. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the weekend work as evidence that the previous capacity plan was insufficient, estimate the new work realistically, and adjust scope, staffing or dates instead of budgeting unpaid recovery time again.$qb$, $qb$Keep the same plan but warn the team earlier that some weekend work may be needed.$qb$, $qb$Ask who is willing to work weekends before finalizing the schedule.$qb$, $qb$Reduce quality checks so weekend work is less likely.$qb$, $qb$Use the previous plan because it ultimately delivered successfully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-431$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure lists a contact person's name for escalation, but that person has moved to another role. The escalation responsibility still exists. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the current role that owns escalation, update the procedure to use the durable role or function rather than only a person's name, and notify users of the change.$qb$, $qb$Replace the old name with the new person's name.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team verbally who the new contact is.$qb$, $qb$Leave the procedure until the next scheduled review.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to search the directory if the named person no longer handles the issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-433$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A small internal event starts in an hour and the ordered name badges have not arrived. You have a printer, blank cards, and the attendee list. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create simple temporary badges from the verified attendee list, keep any required identification controls, and use the ordered badges later if they arrive.$qb$, $qb$Wait another 30 minutes for the supplier before doing anything.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees to write their own names on paper when they arrive.$qb$, $qb$Cancel badge use because it is only a small internal event.$qb$, $qb$Print the attendee list and ask people to point to their names at entry.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-453$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member volunteers for extra work after several busy weeks, but you know they have already been working unusually long hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss their current workload and recovery honestly, assess whether the extra work is sustainable, and find another capacity solution if accepting the offer would extend an unhealthy pattern.$qb$, $qb$Accept the help but ask them to take time off after the deadline.$qb$, $qb$Decline the offer immediately because they have already worked enough.$qb$, $qb$Accept because they volunteered and therefore understand their own capacity.$qb$, $qb$Give them the work but reduce quality expectations to make it manageable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-477$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process document says a review is required 'regularly,' and teams are using intervals ranging from monthly to annually. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended frequency or risk-based rule with the process owner, replace 'regularly' with a measurable requirement, and communicate the clarified standard.$qb$, $qb$Choose a quarterly review for your team because it is a reasonable middle ground.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of monthly and annual reviews so teams can choose.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording because different teams may need different intervals.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to decide the frequency locally without changing the document.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-479$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A key meeting participant loses internet access just before a decision meeting. They can still join by phone but cannot see the shared material. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send or describe the essential decision material in an accessible approved format, adjust the meeting so the participant can follow it, and proceed if meaningful participation remains possible.$qb$, $qb$Continue normally and ask the participant to listen by phone.$qb$, $qb$Postpone the meeting until their internet is restored.$qb$, $qb$Ask another participant to make the decision on their behalf.$qb$, $qb$Remove them from the decision because technical access is their responsibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-499$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has started skipping breaks to recover a schedule delay. Output is improving, but small errors are also increasing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the error increase as a capacity and fatigue signal, reassess the recovery plan, protect reasonable breaks and critical checks, and adjust scope, support or timing if needed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the recovery effort but remind everyone to check their work more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Require a short break once per day while keeping the rest of the plan unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Accept some small errors until the schedule is recovered.$qb$, $qb$Ask people who feel tired to opt out while others continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-523$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A checklist says supporting evidence should be retained but does not say where or for how long. Teams are storing it inconsistently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the approved retention period and location with the policy owner, update the checklist with both requirements, and communicate the clarified rule.$qb$, $qb$Choose the most secure shared folder and tell your team to keep evidence there.$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to document its own retention approach.$qb$, $qb$Keep evidence indefinitely so no record is lost.$qb$, $qb$Leave the checklist unchanged because retention rules may exist elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-525$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A training session starts soon and the prepared exercise file will not open. You have the exercise instructions and can recreate a simpler example quickly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Build a minimal replacement that tests the same learning objective, explain the change briefly, and continue the session while preserving the original file for later troubleshooting.$qb$, $qb$Spend the first part of the session trying to repair the original file.$qb$, $qb$Skip the exercise and continue with lecture content.$qb$, $qb$Ask participants to wait while another colleague recreates the full file.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the session because the planned exercise is unavailable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-545$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague has taken on extra work for several weeks and now says they cannot take planned leave because the team depends on them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the dependency and workload, arrange coverage or reprioritization so leave can be taken where possible, and address why the team has become dependent on one person's continued availability.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to postpone leave until the busy period ends.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to take leave and let the team manage without preparation.$qb$, $qb$Offer to pay or reward them for cancelling leave.$qb$, $qb$Accept their choice because they volunteered to stay available.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-569$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a task must be completed 'promptly,' and teams disagree whether that means hours or days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended service or risk requirement, replace the vague term with a measurable timeframe or defined trigger, and communicate the clarified expectation.$qb$, $qb$Set a 24-hour target for your own team.$qb$, $qb$Tell teams to use reasonable professional judgment.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of both same-day and three-day completion.$qb$, $qb$Leave the wording because exact deadlines can reduce flexibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-571$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A scheduled demonstration relies on a test account that becomes locked minutes before the session. You have screenshots and a second approved test environment with slightly different data. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved alternate environment if it demonstrates the same objective, explain any relevant data difference, and keep screenshots as backup while the locked account is resolved later.$qb$, $qb$Spend the opening of the session trying to unlock the original account.$qb$, $qb$Use screenshots only and cancel all live demonstration.$qb$, $qb$Create a new personal account quickly outside the approved environment.$qb$, $qb$Postpone the demonstration because the exact test account is unavailable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-591$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team is entering a busy month after already carrying unused leave and several weeks of overtime. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review workload, leave and capacity before the peak, identify what can be deferred or covered differently, and protect realistic recovery rather than assuming previous overtime can continue.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to postpone leave until the busy month ends.$qb$, $qb$Offer overtime payments so people are willing to work the extra hours.$qb$, $qb$Keep the plan and encourage people to rest more on weekends.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the busy month is actually as demanding as forecast.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-615$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A policy summary uses the word 'may' where the underlying policy says an action 'must' be taken. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the summary to preserve the mandatory requirement, verify other material wording against the policy, and notify users who may have relied on the weaker wording.$qb$, $qb$Change 'may' to 'must' and leave the rest of the summary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Tell users verbally that the action is actually mandatory.$qb$, $qb$Leave the summary because readers should refer to the full policy for exact wording.$qb$, $qb$Add a disclaimer that summaries are not authoritative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-617$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer workshop needs printed materials, but the delivery is missing. The same material is available digitally and the room has approved display equipment. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Adapt the exercises for digital display or participant devices, provide only essential handwritten or locally printable material if needed, and continue the workshop without waiting for the shipment.$qb$, $qb$Delay the workshop until someone can obtain printed copies.$qb$, $qb$Show the digital material but skip any exercise designed for paper.$qb$, $qb$Ask participants to print the material themselves before the workshop starts.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the interactive parts and deliver a presentation instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-637$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-performing colleague has stopped taking breaks and says they prefer to finish work early, but they are increasingly irritable in afternoon meetings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the observable pattern privately, explore whether their work routine is affecting energy and collaboration, and agree a sustainable adjustment without assuming one specific break pattern fits everyone.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they must take scheduled breaks every day.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because they remain high-performing.$qb$, $qb$Move their meetings to the morning so irritability is less visible.$qb$, $qb$Tell teammates to be patient because the colleague is working hard.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-661$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process guide lists two different deadlines in separate sections because one section was updated and the other was missed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the correct deadline, update both sections and version information, search for related references, and notify users who may have relied on the inconsistent guide.$qb$, $qb$Correct the section most people use.$qb$, $qb$Add a note at the top saying the newer deadline takes priority.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team which deadline is correct without changing the guide.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next scheduled document review to fix both sections.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-663$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer call requires a document stored on a system that is temporarily unavailable. You have an approved PDF copy from yesterday and know no changes were expected overnight. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved copy only after checking its date and whether any change could matter, state the version if relevant, and reconcile with the live system once available.$qb$, $qb$Use yesterday's copy without mentioning the date because no changes were expected.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the call until the live system returns.$qb$, $qb$Ask a colleague to send a screenshot from their personal copy.$qb$, $qb$Recreate the document from memory for the customer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-683$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague has been covering an absent teammate for several weeks and says they can continue, but their normal work is beginning to slip. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the combined workload and slipping commitments, arrange sustainable redistribution or temporary support, and avoid treating willingness to continue as proof that capacity is adequate.$qb$, $qb$Ask them which of their normal tasks they want to postpone.$qb$, $qb$Let them continue because they have not asked for help.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to work more efficiently until the teammate returns.$qb$, $qb$Move all their normal work to other colleagues immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-707$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A form asks users to select 'high risk' or 'low risk,' but the supporting procedure does not define the difference. Teams are classifying similar cases differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the risk criteria with the policy owner, add measurable indicators or decision rules to the procedure, and align current users on the clarified classification.$qb$, $qb$Give your team examples of what you consider high and low risk.$qb$, $qb$Add a medium-risk category so difficult cases have somewhere to go.$qb$, $qb$Let managers classify cases based on professional judgment.$qb$, $qb$Keep the form unchanged because risk can never be defined precisely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-709$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A barcode scanner fails during a small stock check. The approved inventory system allows manual entry, but it is slower. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use manual entry with the required verification for the limited stock check, record the scanner issue, and continue rather than delaying the whole activity for the preferred device.$qb$, $qb$Postpone the stock check until the scanner is repaired.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone scanning app to speed up entry.$qb$, $qb$Estimate quantities and correct them later when the scanner returns.$qb$, $qb$Skip verification because manual entry already takes longer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-729$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team is meeting targets, but several people have accumulated significant unused leave because workloads never seem to ease. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the leave pattern as a capacity signal, plan coverage and workload so leave can realistically be taken, and address structural demand rather than assuming target achievement means capacity is healthy.$qb$, $qb$Encourage everyone to book leave whenever they feel ready.$qb$, $qb$Pay out unused leave where policy allows.$qb$, $qb$Wait for a quieter period before discussing leave.$qb$, $qb$Keep the workload because performance targets are being met.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-753$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a manager can approve an exception, but there are several manager levels and teams use different ones. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required authority level or decision threshold with the policy owner, update the procedure precisely, and align teams on the clarified approval rule.$qb$, $qb$Use the employee's direct manager for all exceptions.$qb$, $qb$Use the most senior available manager to be safe.$qb$, $qb$Let each department choose the appropriate manager level.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording flexible because exception severity varies.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-755$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A routine inspection needs a measuring device that another team is using. A second approved device is available but you have not used that model before. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Check the approved operating guidance or get a quick competent orientation, verify the device is suitable and calibrated, and use it if you can perform the inspection correctly.$qb$, $qb$Wait until your familiar device is returned.$qb$, $qb$Use the second device immediately because it is approved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to interrupt its work and return your usual device.$qb$, $qb$Estimate the measurement visually and confirm it later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-775$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has covered a vacancy for two months by distributing the work among existing employees. Targets are still being met, but overtime is steadily increasing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the overtime trend as evidence that the temporary arrangement is not cost-free, reassess hiring or temporary support, and adjust workload before sustained overcapacity becomes normal.$qb$, $qb$Keep the arrangement because targets are still being met.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to reduce overtime while keeping the same workload.$qb$, $qb$Offer overtime payments and continue until the vacancy is filled.$qb$, $qb$Wait until targets begin to slip before changing the plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-799$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A guide states that records should be retained for 'at least a few years.' Different teams are choosing different periods. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the approved retention rule, replace the vague phrase with the exact period and trigger, and align storage guidance with that requirement.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to keep records for five years to be safe.$qb$, $qb$Keep records indefinitely.$qb$, $qb$Let each team choose a reasonable period based on risk.$qb$, $qb$Add examples of three, five and seven years.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-801$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A room booked for a workshop becomes unavailable because of a facilities issue. Another smaller room is free, and the group can fit if the exercises are adjusted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move to the available room, adapt the exercises to the space while preserving the workshop objectives, and communicate the location change clearly.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the workshop because the planned room is unavailable.$qb$, $qb$Delay the workshop until a similar-sized room is found.$qb$, $qb$Use the smaller room but run the original exercises even if crowded.$qb$, $qb$Move the workshop online without checking whether participants have the required setup.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-821$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member regularly volunteers for weekend work and is praised for reliability, but weekday concentration is beginning to decline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the observable concentration and workload pattern, review why weekend work is recurring, and rebalance capacity rather than reinforcing the pattern through praise alone.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to stop volunteering for weekends immediately.$qb$, $qb$Keep praising the commitment but suggest more sleep.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their weekday workload so weekend work can continue.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because the weekend work is voluntary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-845$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A work instruction says an exception must be approved 'before processing where possible.' Teams interpret this differently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the actual exception rule, define when prior approval is mandatory and any legitimate emergency route, and replace the vague qualifier with explicit conditions.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to always obtain approval before processing.$qb$, $qb$Allow managers to decide when prior approval is possible.$qb$, $qb$Remove 'where possible' and require prior approval in every case.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording because exceptions need flexibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-847$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A small field team loses access to its normal shared folder during a site visit. Approved offline copies of the required checklists were synchronized that morning. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the current approved offline copies, record any updates locally as required, and reconcile them with the shared system once access returns.$qb$, $qb$Stop work until the shared folder is available.$qb$, $qb$Use an older printed checklist from the vehicle.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone in the office to send photos of the files through a personal messaging app.$qb$, $qb$Continue from memory and document the checks later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-867$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has maintained output by repeatedly cancelling internal learning time whenever workload rises. Skill gaps are now starting to slow work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the skill gaps as a consequence of chronic capacity choices, protect targeted learning time, and adjust workload or priorities so capability development is not always the first activity removed.$qb$, $qb$Cancel more learning until workload stabilizes.$qb$, $qb$Add training outside normal hours.$qb$, $qb$Keep output targets and ask employees to learn independently.$qb$, $qb$Wait until performance drops further before restoring learning time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-937$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A policy guide names 'the portal,' but two different portals now handle different request types.$qb$, $qb$Specify the correct portal for each request type, add the routing rule and current links, and remove the ambiguous reference.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team verbally.$qb$, $qb$Use the newer portal everywhere.$qb$, $qb$List both links without guidance.$qb$, $qb$Let users choose.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-939$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A company camera fails during a site visit; another approved camera can be borrowed immediately.$qb$, $qb$Verify the alternate device is suitable and available, use it under the same evidence rules, and continue the visit.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the visit.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone.$qb$, $qb$Take notes and recreate photos later.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the original camera to be repaired.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-959$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project team has started scheduling routine calls at 7 a.m. because daytime calendars are full, and attendance is gradually becoming expected. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the pattern as a capacity and scheduling problem, protect reasonable recovery time, and change workload or meeting design rather than normalizing lost breaks.$qb$, $qb$Keep lunch meetings but provide food.$qb$, $qb$Ask people to take breaks later.$qb$, $qb$Make attendance optional.$qb$, $qb$Wait until performance drops.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-982$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A complaint is technically resolved, but a refund promised to the customer has not been processed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the recovery action open until the refund is confirmed and close only when the promised remedy is complete.$qb$, $qb$Close because the technical problem is fixed.$qb$, $qb$Ask finance to handle it and close your case.$qb$, $qb$Tell the customer to contact finance.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the customer to ask.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-983$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A checklist asks for 'supporting proof' but gives no standard, so teams attach very different evidence.$qb$, $qb$Define the evidence purpose, acceptable examples and minimum criteria, then update the checklist for consistent use.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team what you prefer.$qb$, $qb$Require every possible document.$qb$, $qb$Let reviewers decide individually.$qb$, $qb$Remove the proof requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-985$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A meeting-room display fails, but all participants can access the approved shared document on their laptops.$qb$, $qb$Switch to the shared document and continue the session while reporting the display fault separately.$qb$, $qb$Delay until repaired.$qb$, $qb$Print copies before continuing.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the visual part.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal sharing service.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1005$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Routine 7 a.m. calls are becoming expected because daytime calendars are full.$qb$, $qb$Treat the pattern as a scheduling and capacity problem, redesign meeting load or workload, and protect reasonable recovery rather than normalizing early calls.$qb$, $qb$Keep the calls but make them shorter.$qb$, $qb$Make attendance optional.$qb$, $qb$Ask people to start work earlier.$qb$, $qb$Wait until performance declines.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2843$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new process is live, but the temporary training mailbox created for launch still receives questions that should now go to normal support. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redirect users to normal support, close or repurpose the temporary mailbox with clear communication, and ensure outstanding questions are handed over before closure.$qb$, $qb$Keep the mailbox open indefinitely because users know it.$qb$, $qb$Close it immediately without reviewing pending messages.$qb$, $qb$Ask the training team to keep answering questions.$qb$, $qb$Ignore messages until users discover normal support themselves.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2844$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says records must be stored in a 'secure location' but does not identify approved systems. Teams are using different places. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm approved storage locations and access requirements, update the procedure with explicit options, and move records from unsuitable locations where needed.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team to use the folder you consider safest.$qb$, $qb$Ask each manager to choose a secure location.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording because security needs vary.$qb$, $qb$Require all records to be stored locally on employee computers.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2845$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A small team needs to collect signatures at an event, but the usual tablet fails. Approved paper forms are available as a contingency. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved paper forms, preserve required identity and record controls, and enter or reconcile them into the system afterward as required.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone to collect signatures digitally.$qb$, $qb$Skip signatures and record names only.$qb$, $qb$Delay entry until the tablet is repaired.$qb$, $qb$Ask attendees to email confirmation later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2849$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been covering a vacancy by working through breaks and staying late. Output remains high, but sickness absence is starting to rise. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the absence and workload pattern as a sustainability signal, reassess capacity and priorities, and arrange support rather than relying on continued extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Keep the arrangement because output is high.$qb$, $qb$Tell employees to take better care of themselves outside work.$qb$, $qb$Offer overtime pay and continue.$qb$, $qb$Wait until output falls before changing the plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1120$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer issue is fixed, but a temporary manual check introduced during the incident is still being performed on every transaction. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review whether the permanent fix meets the control need, confirm exit criteria, and retire the temporary check if it is no longer necessary while monitoring normal controls.$qb$, $qb$Keep the manual check because extra control is safer.$qb$, $qb$Stop the check immediately because the incident is closed.$qb$, $qb$Reduce the check to random transactions without reviewing its purpose.$qb$, $qb$Let individual staff decide whether to continue it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1121$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A policy says evidence must be retained for seven years, but the procedure does not state whether the period starts at creation, approval, or case closure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the retention trigger with the policy owner, state it precisely in the procedure, and align current records to the clarified rule.$qb$, $qb$Start seven years from creation because that is easiest to measure.$qb$, $qb$Use case closure because it gives the longest retention.$qb$, $qb$Let each team choose the trigger that fits its process.$qb$, $qb$Keep the procedure unchanged because the seven-year period is already clear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1123$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A small site inspection requires a standard checklist, but the printed copies were left at the office. The approved checklist is available on the company intranet from a tablet. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved current checklist on the tablet, ensure required evidence is captured correctly, and continue the inspection without returning solely for paper copies.$qb$, $qb$Drive back for the printed copies because that is the usual method.$qb$, $qb$Use an old paper copy found at the site.$qb$, $qb$Perform the inspection from memory and complete the checklist later.$qb$, $qb$Download the checklist to a personal device.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1143$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been using unused leave days to cope with workload peaks, and several employees now have large leave balances near year-end. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the leave accumulation as a capacity-planning signal, arrange coverage and reprioritization so leave can be taken, and address the workload pattern causing repeated deferral.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to carry the leave forward if policy allows.$qb$, $qb$Tell everyone to book leave immediately without changing workload.$qb$, $qb$Pay out leave and keep the current workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait until year-end and deal with balances individually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1166$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project is ready to close, but one open action has been transferred to operations without a named owner. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assign and confirm an operational owner, due date and tracking location before closing the project, so the action does not disappear during handover.$qb$, $qb$Close the project because operations accepted the action.$qb$, $qb$Keep the entire project open until the action is finished.$qb$, $qb$Take the action yourself after project closure.$qb$, $qb$Put the action in meeting notes without an owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1167$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A contract checklist says to confirm 'adequate insurance' but gives no required coverage level. Reviewers are applying different standards. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required coverage types and thresholds with the contract or risk owner, then update the checklist with measurable criteria.$qb$, $qb$Use the highest coverage you have seen on previous contracts.$qb$, $qb$Let each reviewer decide what adequate means.$qb$, $qb$Ask suppliers to provide whatever insurance they normally carry.$qb$, $qb$Remove the insurance check because the standard is unclear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1169$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to label several samples, but the label printer stops working. Approved blank labels and permanent markers are available under the contingency procedure. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the approved manual labeling method, apply the required identifiers and verification, and reconcile or reprint labels later if the procedure requires it.$qb$, $qb$Stop sampling until the printer is repaired.$qb$, $qb$Write abbreviated labels to save time.$qb$, $qb$Use ordinary sticky notes instead.$qb$, $qb$Leave samples unlabeled and identify them later from memory.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1189$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team is hitting deadlines by regularly postponing training and one-to-one development conversations. Capability gaps are now appearing in new work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat development debt as a delivery risk, protect targeted learning and coaching time, and reprioritize workload so capability is not repeatedly sacrificed to short-term deadlines.$qb$, $qb$Keep postponing development until workload reduces.$qb$, $qb$Move all training outside working hours.$qb$, $qb$Add more deadlines so employees learn faster through experience.$qb$, $qb$Wait until performance failures become serious.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1212$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has delivered its final output, but a recurring automated job created for the project still runs and sends reports nobody owns.$qb$, $qb$Identify whether the job is still needed, assign an operational owner if it is, or disable it safely and confirm no downstream dependency before project closure.$qb$, $qb$Leave it running because automation costs little.$qb$, $qb$Disable it immediately without checking dependencies.$qb$, $qb$Keep receiving reports in the project mailbox.$qb$, $qb$Ask IT to decide whether it matters.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1213$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure tells staff to use the 'latest approved template' but does not state where the approved template is stored.$qb$, $qb$Name the authoritative location, link it directly in the procedure, and remove or archive competing copies that can reasonably confuse users.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team which folder to use.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to search by most recent file date.$qb$, $qb$Attach a copy to every email request.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because staff should know the document library.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1215$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A field visit requires recording temperatures, but the usual digital device loses power. An approved calibrated manual thermometer is available.$qb$, $qb$Use the approved manual instrument, record readings and device details as required, and continue while arranging the digital device issue separately.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the visit.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal consumer thermometer.$qb$, $qb$Estimate temperatures from previous visits.$qb$, $qb$Record no temperatures and add them later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1235$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has met a demanding deadline for three consecutive months by cancelling planned leave and working weekends.$qb$, $qb$Treat the repeated recovery effort as evidence that normal capacity is insufficient, restore realistic recovery time, and change workload, staffing or commitments before the pattern becomes standard.$qb$, $qb$Celebrate the team's commitment and continue until the busy period ends.$qb$, $qb$Offer more overtime pay.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to take leave only after deadlines are easier.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone refuses weekend work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1258$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has closed its technical work, but several users still have temporary elevated access granted during implementation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review who still needs elevated access, remove temporary permissions that are no longer justified, confirm any continuing access has an operational owner, and then close the access action.$qb$, $qb$Leave access because users may need it later.$qb$, $qb$Remove everyone's access immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to tell you if they no longer need access.$qb$, $qb$Close the project and let IT review access eventually.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1259$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a document must be reviewed 'regularly' but gives no review interval or trigger. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the intended risk-based review frequency or event triggers, state them explicitly, and assign ownership so the requirement can be demonstrated consistently.$qb$, $qb$Review it once a year because that is common.$qb$, $qb$Let each owner decide what regular means.$qb$, $qb$Review only when a problem occurs.$qb$, $qb$Remove the review requirement because the timing is unclear.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1261$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A delivery team arrives at a site and finds the normal loading bay closed. A second approved bay is open but requires a different documented route. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the alternate bay is authorized, follow the documented route and safety requirements, and continue the delivery while reporting the closure through the normal channel.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the usual bay reopens.$qb$, $qb$Use the second bay but take the familiar route.$qb$, $qb$Unload at an unofficial nearby entrance.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the delivery without checking the approved alternative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1281$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been meeting targets by leaving several vacancies unfilled and distributing the work among existing staff. Quality complaints are beginning to increase. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Connect the complaint trend to capacity and workload evidence, reassess vacancy and priority decisions, and restore sustainable capacity before quality deteriorates further.$qb$, $qb$Keep the vacancies open because targets are still being met.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff to focus more carefully on quality.$qb$, $qb$Add extra quality checks without changing workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait until targets are missed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1304$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A temporary project phone number is still published on customer material after the project team has disbanded. Calls now go unanswered. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redirect or remove the number, update customer material and ownership, and verify active channels before treating the project communication setup as closed.$qb$, $qb$Ask a former project member to check the phone occasionally.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because customers should use the main number.$qb$, $qb$Disconnect the number immediately without updating material.$qb$, $qb$Wait until customers complain.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1305$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a case must be escalated to a 'senior manager' but the organization has several different manager grades. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the required authority level or decision threshold, name it precisely in the procedure, and align teams on the same escalation route.$qb$, $qb$Use the most senior manager available.$qb$, $qb$Use the employee's direct manager.$qb$, $qb$Let each department define senior manager locally.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording flexible so difficult cases can go higher.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1307$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to scan asset tags, but the approved scanner is temporarily unavailable. The asset numbers are clearly printed and the system allows verified manual entry. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use manual entry with the required verification for the limited task, record the scanner issue, and continue rather than waiting solely for the preferred device.$qb$, $qb$Delay the asset check until the scanner returns.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal scanning application.$qb$, $qb$Enter numbers without verification to save time.$qb$, $qb$Estimate asset numbers from the previous inventory.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1327$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has met targets for months by asking the same few high performers to take extra work whenever demand spikes. Those employees are now taking more unplanned leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the concentration of extra work and leave pattern as a sustainability risk, rebalance skills and workload, and build broader capacity rather than relying on the same people.$qb$, $qb$Keep using the high performers because they deliver reliably.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to take planned leave only during quiet periods.$qb$, $qb$Pay them more for extra work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until their performance drops.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1350$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A temporary customer FAQ created for a launch remains online months later and now conflicts with the normal support page.$qb$, $qb$Review any still-useful content, retire or redirect the temporary FAQ, and confirm customers reach the current authoritative guidance.$qb$, $qb$Leave both pages online because more information helps.$qb$, $qb$Delete the temporary page immediately without checking useful content.$qb$, $qb$Ask support to explain the difference whenever customers call.$qb$, $qb$Wait for complaints before changing it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1351$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure requires 'prompt escalation' but gives no time or event trigger, so teams act differently.$qb$, $qb$Define the risk or event conditions and expected escalation timing with the owner, then update the procedure so the requirement is measurable.$qb$, $qb$Tell your team prompt means within one hour.$qb$, $qb$Let each manager decide what prompt means.$qb$, $qb$Escalate every issue immediately.$qb$, $qb$Remove the word prompt and leave the rest unchanged.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1353$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site visit requires signed attendance, but the usual electronic sign-in device is unavailable and approved paper attendance sheets are on site.$qb$, $qb$Use the approved paper sheet with required identity checks and reconcile it into the normal record afterward.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the visit.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone form.$qb$, $qb$Let people attend without signing and reconstruct attendance later.$qb$, $qb$Ask participants to email confirmation after the visit.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1373$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team has been covering demand spikes by repeatedly asking employees to postpone leave at short notice.$qb$, $qb$Treat repeated leave disruption as a capacity and planning signal, improve coverage and workload planning, and protect reasonable leave reliability.$qb$, $qb$Keep postponing leave because spikes are temporary.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to book only quiet periods.$qb$, $qb$Pay extra for cancelled leave.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone refuses.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1396$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has ended, but an automated weekly meeting invitation created for the project continues to invite twenty people. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Cancel the recurring series, preserve any needed final record, and confirm no continuing operational meeting depends on it before closing the project calendar setup.$qb$, $qb$Let people decline the meetings themselves.$qb$, $qb$Keep the series in case the team needs to reconnect.$qb$, $qb$Delete only your own calendar copy.$qb$, $qb$Wait until attendance falls to zero.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1397$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A work instruction says a reviewer must have 'sufficient experience' but gives no qualification or competency standard. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the capability required for the review, define objective eligibility or authorization criteria, and update the instruction so assignments are consistent.$qb$, $qb$Use employees with at least five years of service.$qb$, $qb$Let managers decide who seems experienced enough.$qb$, $qb$Use only the most senior employee available.$qb$, $qb$Remove the experience requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1399$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team needs to verify package weights, but the usual scale is being calibrated. Another approved calibrated scale is available in a nearby area. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the alternate scale is suitable for the weight range, use it under the same recording procedure, and continue the work.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the usual scale because staff know it better.$qb$, $qb$Use an uncalibrated desk scale temporarily.$qb$, $qb$Estimate weights from package contents.$qb$, $qb$Skip weight verification for this batch.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1419$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly handles workload peaks by cancelling internal improvement work. Small process problems are now accumulating and consuming more time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the growing process debt as a capacity issue, protect targeted improvement time, and reprioritize workload so recurring inefficiencies are not continually deferred.$qb$, $qb$Keep cancelling improvement work because customer delivery comes first.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to improve processes outside normal work.$qb$, $qb$Launch a large improvement project without reducing workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait until a major failure justifies attention.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1442$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project is complete, but a temporary distribution list still sends internal updates to former project members. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Remove or update the obsolete distribution list after confirming any continuing operational recipients, and stop project updates from going to former members.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because extra recipients cause little harm.$qb$, $qb$Ask former members to unsubscribe themselves.$qb$, $qb$Delete the list immediately without checking ongoing use.$qb$, $qb$Keep it for six months as a precaution.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1443$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says a case needs 'management approval' without defining which manager or authority level. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the required authority level or decision threshold and update the procedure so teams know exactly who can approve.$qb$, $qb$Use the direct manager for every case.$qb$, $qb$Use the most senior available manager.$qb$, $qb$Let departments interpret 'management' locally.$qb$, $qb$Keep the wording flexible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1445$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A workshop needs participants to mark locations on a large floor plan. The usual printed poster was damaged, but the approved plan can be displayed on a touchscreen that supports annotations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the alternate gauge is approved and suitable for the measurement range, then use it under the normal recording process.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the usual gauge.$qb$, $qb$Use any available measuring tool.$qb$, $qb$Estimate from previous readings.$qb$, $qb$Skip the measurement and add it later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1465$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team keeps using planned process-improvement afternoons to clear routine backlog, and small recurring inefficiencies are steadily increasing the daily workload. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the growing process problems as evidence that improvement work cannot be deferred indefinitely, and reprioritize workload to protect targeted improvement time.$qb$, $qb$Keep cancelling improvement days during busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Ask staff to improve processes after hours.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a large improvement event without reducing workload.$qb$, $qb$Wait for a major failure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1488$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project is finished, but a temporary dashboard created for launch is still being refreshed manually every day. Normal operational reporting now covers the same information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the touchscreen meets workshop security and visibility requirements, display the approved plan, and save annotated outputs directly to the shared project repository.$qb$, $qb$Delay the workshop until a replacement paper poster can be printed.$qb$, $qb$Display the plan on individual laptops and ask participants to take personal notes.$qb$, $qb$Sketch a simplified version of the floor plan on a whiteboard.$qb$, $qb$Proceed with the workshop verbally without displaying any floor plan.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1489$qb$, $qb$A7-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A procedure says evidence must be 'recent' but gives no maximum age. Reviewers are accepting documents from very different periods. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the acceptable age or event-based validity with the policy owner and update the procedure so reviewers apply one standard.$qb$, $qb$Use documents from the last year because that seems recent.$qb$, $qb$Let each reviewer judge relevance.$qb$, $qb$Accept only documents dated this month.$qb$, $qb$Remove the recency requirement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1491$qb$, $qb$A7-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A site team needs to photograph equipment labels, but the usual company camera is being repaired. Another approved company tablet has a suitable camera. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the tablet meets the evidence and security requirements, use it under the normal file-handling process, and continue the inspection.$qb$, $qb$Delay until the normal camera returns.$qb$, $qb$Use a personal phone and email the photos later.$qb$, $qb$Write down the labels instead of photographing them.$qb$, $qb$Skip the evidence and rely on the inspection notes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1511$qb$, $qb$A7-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team repeatedly uses its most experienced employee to cover absences, and that employee has started postponing their own development and leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the repeated dependency as a resilience risk, cross-train others, rebalance cover, and protect the experienced employee's development and recovery time.$qb$, $qb$Keep using them because they are the most reliable cover.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to take development outside busy periods.$qb$, $qb$Pay them extra for covering absences.$qb$, $qb$Wait until they refuse additional cover.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1534$qb$, $qb$A7-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has ended, but a temporary daily email alert created during rollout is still going to managers even though normal dashboards now show the same information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the dashboard covers the alert's purpose, notify recipients of the normal source, and retire the temporary email alert.$qb$, $qb$Keep the alert because managers may prefer email.$qb$, $qb$Stop it immediately without checking dashboard coverage.$qb$, $qb$Reduce it to weekly emails.$qb$, $qb$Let each manager decide whether the alert continues.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
